:"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"blind_id\":\"HOX008\",\"coverage\":[{\"point\":1,\"credit\":1,\"reason\":\"States that excess reportable income (reportable income minus actual distributions) is 'deemed distributed' and taxed 'despite receiving no cash'. This matches the HMRC excerpt: investors are taxed on both actual distributions and undistributed income.\"},{\"point\":2,\"credit\":1,\"reason\":\"States 'Gains face capital gains tax, not income tax' and 'This assumes reporting status throughout ownership.' Capital-gains treatment conditioned on reporting status throughout ownership meets the minimum.\"},{\"point\":3,\"credit\":0,\"reason\":\"Optional point. The answer does not mention offshore income gain treatment for non-reporting funds. Omitting it does not reduce the grade.\"}],\"central_error\":null,\"local_error\":null,\"grade\":\"correct\",\"rationale\":\"Both required points are fully covered. The answer does not say tax is deferred until sale, and it does not exclude undistributed excess income from reportable income; it affirmatively includes it, so there is no central error. The supplied evidence does not cover the extra details (six-month distribution date, the 60% interest test, the 18%/24% CGT rates, the £3,000 exempt amount, the base-cost uplift), but nothing in it contradicts them. They are not material falsehoods.\"}","ttft_ms":3064,"type":"result","duration_ms":6726,"uuid":"b5cdd668-e574-40cc-b2b5-08ab64bf0e34","ttft_stream_ms":558,"time_to_request_ms":93,"first_content_frame_ms":558,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
